<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Niksar</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Neocaesarea</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Niksar is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokat">Tokat</a> Province, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, on the Kelkit river. As ancient Neocaesarea it was a city of Pontus and the home of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> Gregory Thaumaturgus. In the late 11th century it fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danishmendids">Danishmendids</a>, for whom it was an early capital, and their mosques, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a> and tombs survive in the town.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/niksar.jpg" alt="Niksar" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bertramz · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Niksar,KaleSW.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Niksar is the seat of Niksar District in Tokat Province, at an average altitude of 350 metres. It lies between the Canik Mountains to the north and Dönek Mountain to the south, in the Niksar Lowland, one of the most important lowlands of the Black Sea Region. Tributaries of the Kelkit River irrigate its land, about 32 per cent of which is farmed. The town is known for walnuts, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomato">tomato</a> paste and grape leaves, the main ingredient of dolma. It has always counted as an important place in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> because of its location, climate and productive farmland.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The town has been ruled by Hittite, Persian, Greek, Pontic, Roman, Byzantine, Danishmend, Seljuk and Ottoman empires. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a> it was called Cabira and was a favourite residence of Mithridates VI, who built a palace there, and later of King Polemon I and his successors.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cabira">Battle of Cabira</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-mithridatic-war">Third Mithridatic War</a> was fought there in 72 or 71 BCE, and the city passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>. Pompey made it a city and named it Diopolis, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pythodoris">Pythodoris</a>, widow of Polemon, made it her capital under the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Sebaste</a>. It was called Neocaesarea in Roman times, perhaps from the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a>, judging by its coins. An earthquake destroyed it in 344.</p><p>In the Middle Ages Muslims and Christians disputed it. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manzikert">Battle of Manzikert</a> it changed hands several times: Seljuk commanders took it, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> retook it, and for a time it belonged to the Norman mercenary Roussel de Bailleul, who held it until 1075. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danishmend-gazi">Danishmend Gazi</a>, founder of the Danishmend dynasty, then conquered it and made it his capital, and as Niksar it became a centre of science and culture.</p><p>His mausoleum stands in a large cemetery just outside the town. Bohemond I of Taranto was held prisoner in Niksar castle from 1100 to 1103. Later it was governed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> of Rum, the Eretnids and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">Beylik</a> of Tacettin. After Kadı Burhanettin was killed, Süleyman Çelebi took it for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-i">Bayezid I</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Neocaesarea was a bishopric of the late Roman province of Pontus Polemoniacus and the civil and religious metropolis of Pontus. About 240 Gregory Thaumaturgus became its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, and according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-nyssa">Gregory of Nyssa</a> the city then had only seventeen Christians and at his death in 270 only seventeen pagans. A council met there around 315. Paul of Neocaesarea and Thomas, a martyr of the 9th century, were also bishops. The Annuario Pontificio lists it as a titular see, Neocaesarea in Ponto. Niksar was added to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>'s World Heritage tentative list in 2018.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Niksar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Seljuk victory at Manzikert in 1071, a Turkmen leader known as Danishmend Gazi, whose origins are obscure, conquered Sivas, Tokat, <b>Niksar</b>, Amasya and later Malatya, founding one of the first Turkish principalities in Anatolia.</blockquote><a href="/state/danishmendids">Danishmendids</a></li><li><blockquote>As Eretnid authority weakened in the mid 14th century, local Turkmen chiefs took control of the Pontic hinterland between <b>Niksar</b> and the coast.</blockquote><a href="/state/taceddinids">Taceddinids</a></li></ul></section>
